nodes.command:
# Lists the dependencies with a newer version in their range, in every workspace.
set -e
list=$(mktemp)
bun outdated --recursive --no-progress > "$list" 2>&1 || { cat "$list"; exit 1; }
cat "$list"
awk -F'|' -v branch="deps/$(date +%Y-%m-%d)" '
	/^\| / && $2 !~ /^ *Package/ {
		for (i = 2; i <= 6; i++) gsub(/^ +| +$/, "", $i)
		if ($3 == $4) next
		items = items (n++ ? ", " : "") sprintf("{\"name\": \"%s\", \"current\": \"%s\", \"update\": \"%s\", \"workspace\": \"%s\"}", $2, $3, $4, $6)
		text = text sprintf("- %s: %s to %s (%s)\\n", $2, $3, $4, $6)
	}
	END {
		printf "{\"outcome\": \"%s\", \"count\": %d, \"branch\": \"%s\", \"packages\": [%s], \"list\": \"%s\"}\n", (n ? "outdated" : "none"), n, branch, items, text
	}
' "$list" > "$NS_OUTPUT"
rm -f "$list"

nodes.prompt:
Bump these dependencies to the new version. Each line names the package, its version now, the new version, and its workspace:

{{outdated.data.list}}

Change the version in each package.json, then run bun install so the lockfile changes with them. Read the release notes of a package when its change can break this code, and fix the code where it does. Keep the change to the bumps and the fixes they need. Commit with the message "chore(deps): bump dependencies".

What the checks found on your last try, if anything:
{{checks.data.report}}
